  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nCURRENTS & TONIGHT: Taking a look across the area, overcast skies \npersist for most areas this afternoon as southerly winds continue \nto strengthen. As of 18z (12pm), many observation sites continue to \nrecord wind gusts in the 30-45 mph range with the strongest winds \nacross portions of northwestern IA. With this in mind, the wind \nadvisory in effect through 6 pm looks to be in great shape. From \nhere, we should see surface winds gradually weaken late this evening \nas cold front swings through portions of the area. Looking aloft, \nstrengthening frontal forcing and ample shear could lead to a few \nisolated showers developing west of I-29 as the cold front \nprogresses across the area. However, any accumulations would be \nlight at best. Otherwise, after a few breaks in the clouds this \nevening, we'll see a resurgence of cloud cover overnight especially \neast of I-29. Similar to this morning, can't rule out a few areas of \npatchy drizzle with the lower LCLs and lingering low-level \nsaturation. Lastly, nudged up our overnight lows by blending in some \nCONSshort. With this in mind, most areas will wake up in the upper \n30s to upper 40s by Saturday morning.\n\nSATURDAY AND SUNDAY: Looking into the rest of the weekend, the \ndrearier conditions will persist into Saturday. Drizzle to very \nlight showers will continue east of I-29 to start the day with the \nsurface front. Colder air will begin to filter in with the 850mb \nfront during the afternoon hours. Looking aloft, strengthening mid-\nlevel frontal forcing will lead to additional showers developing \nmainly along and east of Highway-75 by Saturday afternoon. While \nthese showers could continue intermittently through late evening, \nany accumulations will be light. Otherwise, quieter and chillier \nconditions will return into Sunday as an additional push of cold air \nadvection (CAA) filters through the region. This along with \nnorthwesterly surface winds, will keep conditions on the chillier \nside as highs only peak in the mid 30s to low 40s for the day. \nLastly, the coldest conditions will be felt overnight as temperatures\ndip into the upper teens to low 20s for the first time this season.\n\nNEXT WEEK: Heading into the new week, quasi-zonal flow returns by \nMonday as a surface high slides down across the northern plains. \nWhile mid-range guidance slightly differs in timing, we're \ncontinuing to a weak signal for light snow with our the next system. \nWhile there's still some questions about moisture, this system could \nmove through the area between Monday and Tuesday. Either way, any \naccumulations would be very light and focused west of I-29 IF things \ncan develop. Otherwise, expect the colder conditions to persist well \ninto the week with highs mainly in the 30s to low 40s and overnight \nlows in the upper teens to low 20s.",
